  "notes": "A Berklee-trained jazz composer turned interpolation-pop producer for Beluga Heights, 2001–2010; the research calls his \"95–130 straight\" band \"the fastest habitual band among the R&B producers here\", and the mode is 100, the sourced tempo of the D-major record. Three drum deltas from \"a bright, simple kit under an interpolated melody — kick on 1 and 3, clap/snare on 2&4, continuous 16th hats\": the kick anchors on both downbeats with syncopation at 0.1 (the simplest kick placement in this section), the clap sits within 2 ms of the snare on a dead-flat grid, and the hat runs continuous 16ths at 0.68 fill density with open hats on both offbeats — bright, not busy. The research adds a negative that is authored as one: \"his records are identified by a producer tag… rather than by drum complexity\", so `swing.amount` is 0.50, `quantizeStrength` 0.9 and the rolls sit at 0.1. Harmony is the whole method and the research says so: \"**interpolation is the method** — the hook is a re-sung, re-harmonised oldie… placed over a modern beat. His jazz-composition training shows in the re-voicing rather than in the surface harmony, **which stays diatonic I–vi–IV–V**.\" So that family carries a weight of 6, major keys dominate, and `chordToneBias` is 0.9 — the highest in this section, because an interpolated hook sits on the changes by construction. \"Extremely high hook density\" is the arrangement (hook first, hook everywhere) and the 4–9 melody density. The bass is \"808 or synth bass following the interpolated chord roots in 8ths\", so `offbeat_8ths` with repeated-root cells and no `bass808` block. ⚠ Confidence is medium: the method, training, tag and credits are sourced; the drum specifics are inferred. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is part-sourced: the entry names `D`, and the remaining values `C`, `G`, `F`, `A`, together with all 5 weights, are INFERRED rather than read from the entry.",
